Rym: And it gets into and the data is incomplete because there's some questions they didn't ask that Kotaku points out.

218
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:18,520
Rym: But basically, they were doing a study, a real statistical analysis of kids in America aged 10 to 17, which is a very, very hot demographic for capital.

219
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:24,800
Rym: Like that's like one of the most important demographics to get your grubby little meat hooks into as hard as possible.

220
00:13:25,140 --> 00:13:27,940
Scott: Well, because even if those people have no money.

221
00:13:28,451 --> 00:13:28,700
Scott: Right.

222
00:13:28,941 --> 00:13:31,839
Scott: If you can get them to fall in love with your thing.

223
00:13:32,433 --> 00:13:32,640
Scott: Right.

224
00:13:33,181 --> 00:13:36,560
Scott: Then once they have money that they're right.

225
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:41,120
Scott: It's like, you know, Pokemon is the number one intellectual property in history.

226
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:41,660
Scott: Right.

227
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:48,040
Scott: In terms of total monies from all its different things it does, you know, all its different incarnations and products.

228
00:13:49,007 --> 00:13:49,278
Scott: Why?

229
00:13:49,741 --> 00:13:53,820
Scott: Because it got its hooks into a bunch of kids who didn't have any fucking money.

230
00:13:54,282 --> 00:13:57,059
Scott: And then they did have money and they were still Pokemaniacs.

231
00:13:57,482 --> 00:14:00,360
Scott: and it continues to infect constantly.

232
00:14:00,682 --> 00:14:01,620
Scott: It has never let up.

233
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:05,980
Scott: Every new 10 year old is Pokemon every single time.

234
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:06,919
Rym: Think about Disney.

235
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:08,499
Rym: Well, we just talked about the lifers.

236
00:14:08,641 --> 00:14:13,136
Rym: There's definitely some people we know who would not be opposed to having a Pokeball on their tombstone.

237
00:14:15,321 --> 00:14:17,180
Rym: So there's one grain of salt.

238
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:22,580
Rym: This study was commissioned by the ESA, the Entertainment Software Association.

239
00:14:22,881 --> 00:14:27,080
Rym: So there might be a bias toward entertainment software.

240
00:14:27,662 --> 00:14:30,700
Rym: But that said, the study says that in that demographic.

241
00:14:31,340 --> 00:14:34,253
Scott: But they also, you know, the ESA serves its members.

242
00:14:35,921 --> 00:14:38,000
Scott: It wants to provide them with accurate information.

243
00:14:39,086 --> 00:14:46,400
Scott: They can make correct decisions, at least maybe not correct, but best for capitalism decisions for themselves.

244
00:14:47,080 --> 00:14:59,377
Rym: So according to the ESA, kids aged 10 to 17 in America, if they were asked what do they want most for Christmas, 72% of all responses were related to video games.

245
00:15:00,780 --> 00:15:01,419
Scott: Across the board.

246
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:03,399
Rym: Video games is all kids want.

247
00:15:04,842 --> 00:15:07,940
Rym: So the deeper part, because that makes sense.

248
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:13,520
Rym: I mean, talk to a kid in the year 2023 and you would be surprised that number is so low.

249
00:15:13,942 --> 00:15:15,896
Scott: I would have given that same answer in 1980 something.

250
00:15:17,606 --> 00:15:22,320
Rym: At almost any point in my life, I would have said a Nintendo game of some kind when I was young.

251
00:15:22,806 --> 00:15:23,279
Scott: That's right.

252
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:26,920
Rym: But digging in deeper is even more interesting.

253
00:15:28,043 --> 00:15:40,476
Rym: So of the kids who want video games, 39% of them want game subscriptions, which appears to be things like battle passes for games they already play.

254
00:15:41,983 --> 00:15:48,820
Rym: The next one down, 29%, so already we're way over the majority, want currency in a game they already play.

255
00:15:49,203 --> 00:15:49,918
Scott: Yeah, they want robux.

256
00:15:50,683 --> 00:15:52,900
Rym: Kids don't even want new digital games.

257
00:15:53,300 --> 00:16:02,920
Rym: Almost all kids want robux or the equivalent currency or v-bucks or whatever, or they want battle passes for games they otherwise play.

258
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:07,759
Scott: Robux let you buy games in Robux, so it's effectively buying games.

259
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:16,640
Rym: And that's where, as the reporter here points out, the ESA did not release data breaking down what the fuck they mean by game subscriptions.

260
00:16:18,350 --> 00:16:22,380
Rym: There's some explanation, but there are definitely limits to how deep this public dataset goes.

261
00:16:23,063 --> 00:16:34,179
Rym: But it is very interesting that only a very small percentage of kids want actual, brand new games that are not part of an existing, closed ecosystem.

262
00:16:35,462 --> 00:16:38,000
Scott: Yeah, I mean, you get hooked on a thing, right?

263
00:16:39,220 --> 00:16:47,200
Scott: Nowadays, when I'm an adult, our brains, at least my brain, is wired in such a way where I seek novelty and I'm always looking for new and different things.

264
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:48,920
Rym: I like Overwatch, I like Counter-Strike.

265
00:16:49,341 --> 00:16:51,780
Rym: I don't play them both all the time.

266
00:16:51,962 --> 00:16:53,259
Rym: I do play other video games.

267
00:16:54,220 --> 00:17:10,119
Scott: But when I was a kid, I guess I definitely did more, not just because I had less things, and there wasn't such an abundance, but also it's like, yeah, I could watch a VHS tape a thousand times and I could play with the same toy.

268
00:17:10,481 --> 00:17:10,867
Scott: I could do that.

269
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:17,559
Scott: And that was how I played Civilization II over and over, more than I played any other one.

270
00:17:17,819 --> 00:17:21,739
Rym: If I didn't have to work, I would just play all those games more.

271
00:17:22,586 --> 00:17:23,880
Rym: I would just dive in deep.

272
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:30,420
Scott: But if Minecraft existed when I was 10, 12, 13, I would have been fucked.

273
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:34,860
Scott: I probably would have just played Minecraft and I probably would have, right?

274
00:17:35,222 --> 00:17:38,880
Scott: So if they were selling, it's like, why would I want another game?

275
00:17:39,061 --> 00:17:40,660
Scott: I'm still super into Minecraft.

276
00:17:41,080 --> 00:17:42,220
Scott: I just want Minecraft bugs.

277
00:17:42,220 --> 00:17:49,180
Rym: And it's hard to have a strong opinion on exactly how good or bad this is because this is all still pretty new ecosystems.

278
00:17:49,665 --> 00:17:56,879
Rym: All these things we're talking about, these concepts of game development and monetization did not exist on Earth when we were kids.

279
00:17:57,821 --> 00:18:00,500
Rym: So we can't fall for the trap of.

280
00:18:01,207 --> 00:18:03,519
Rym: it was different when we were young and that is a better way.

281
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:20,239
Rym: But simultaneously, I do fear that the way games monetize as capitalism gets ever increasingly extreme is fundamentally breaking some things and I don't know how games that aren't like Roblox and Minecraft will get made in the future.

282
00:18:21,941 --> 00:18:31,520
Scott: I think it's when we were playing games, we were doing mostly PCs and consoles and it's like, you bought a game, you beat it, right?

283
00:18:32,102 --> 00:18:37,470
Rym: I bought Mario 3 for $70 and I definitely got more than 70 1990s American dollars worth of fun out of that.

284
00:18:40,106 --> 00:18:41,360
Scott: Only a few, right?

285
00:18:42,124 --> 00:18:49,655
Scott: They probably got a Switch or they're just using iPads and iPhones and it's like, that's the game.

286
00:18:49,696 --> 00:18:56,899
Scott: they got on there and they don't lose, they don't stop enjoying it and go looking for a different game or they don't think about it, I don't know.

287
00:18:57,140 --> 00:19:06,300
Rym: I hope and again, suspicion, I have no data to back this up, what I suspect is happening is not what a lot of older gamers might say is happening.

288
00:19:07,265 --> 00:19:17,499
Rym: I think a lot of these ecosystems are capturing people who wouldn't have been video gamers or would not have engaged the way that we would expect them to.

289
00:19:18,283 --> 00:19:32,506
Rym: I suspect the market for the kinds of games and experiences that we talk about and like, I want to buy this game for a certain amount of money and play it and enjoy it and move on to another game, I feel like that demographic is also growing.

290
00:19:32,526 --> 00:19:43,217
Rym: This other one just makes more money and is bigger because it's pushed more, but I don't think it's displacing the other side of gaming as much as it might feel like it is.

291
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:47,700
Scott: I think, you know, when I was a kid, my world was small, right?

292
00:19:47,741 --> 00:19:52,407
Scott: The things I knew about and, you know, there wasn't an internet the whole time, right?

293
00:19:52,488 --> 00:19:58,424
Scott: But it's like even when the internet started to come in, you know, around 13, 14, whatever.

294
00:19:58,444 --> 00:20:01,214
Rym: Like the Neo Geo was something that was inaccessible to me.

295
00:20:01,335 --> 00:20:02,267
Rym: I barely knew that existed.

296
00:20:02,287 --> 00:20:02,917
Rym: I didn't touch one.

297
00:20:03,880 --> 00:20:05,740
Scott: Right, I saw pictures of them in video game magazines.

298
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:15,279
Scott: But the world as I understood, you know, the amount of the Earth that was mapped and human society that was mapped in my brain was small.

299
00:20:15,744 --> 00:20:18,167
Scott: So the things that I could want, right?

300
00:20:18,631 --> 00:20:21,320
Scott: You know, it's like I could imagine things that didn't exist.

301
00:20:21,821 --> 00:20:26,228
Scott: But in terms of things that did exist, I didn't know about a lot of things that existed.

302
00:20:26,248 --> 00:20:34,207
Scott: So I, you know, my imagination of what I could even ask for for a holiday as a gift was not a wide variety of things.

303
00:20:34,632 --> 00:20:42,774
Scott: And only when I got older did I start to understand the size of the world and know about all the things and possibilities that are in it.

304
00:20:43,279 --> 00:20:55,744
Scott: So it's that, you know, it's I think kids just even with you know, Internet connected iPads, I'm going to guess kids probably still don't have a very you know, large world view.

305
00:20:55,764 --> 00:20:56,430
Scott: Right.

306
00:20:56,712 --> 00:20:59,619
Scott: And they can only ask for what things that they know about.

307
00:20:59,882 --> 00:21:00,084
Rym: Yeah.

308
00:21:00,125 --> 00:21:19,219
Rym: But I but I do also think especially things like Fortnite you know, V-Bucks and Robux and those types of ecosystems, I think they are directly predatory and may pull some percentage of kids into a permanent lifestyle closed ecosystem and keep that map small in the gaming world.

309
00:21:20,180 --> 00:21:22,069
Scott: Maybe the Robux is definitely child labor.

310
00:21:22,170 --> 00:21:26,192
Rym: Robux is the worst of all of them, I think right now, at least that I'm aware of.

311
00:21:26,212 --> 00:21:28,656
Scott: I don't know if it's the worst because I don't know what other ones exist.

312
00:21:28,737 --> 00:21:30,418
Rym: It's the worst of the big ones that I'm aware of.

313
00:21:31,624 --> 00:21:35,795
Scott: It's one that I'm confidently saying is, yeah, it's definitely bad.

