00:42:14,560 --> 00:42:14,742
Rym: Right.

614
00:42:15,572 --> 00:42:30,723
Scott: Those players are, they're going around and they're basically shipping goods and delivering goods and somewhat interacting with the two main players, you know, being interfered with by them and interfering with them and sometimes helping them.

615
00:42:31,470 --> 00:42:40,239
Scott: And they're just trying to sort of race against, you know, these main players who are sort of a steady point-getting machine.

616
00:42:40,840 --> 00:42:47,900
Scott: The smugglers have to run around, run around, run around, and try to keep up with that machine or outpace that machine with their actions and activities.

617
00:42:48,240 --> 00:42:48,321
Rym: Yup.

618
00:42:48,644 --> 00:43:07,599
Rym: So they're kind of directly competing, and the other two players are directly competing across each other, but where they intersect is really interesting because the area control aspect, where the smugglers are active, those areas become more valuable victory point-wise, so that's one way we interact.

619
00:43:08,845 --> 00:43:19,219
Rym: Two, everyone can shoot at each other, so like, the two main players could just blow the shit out of the smugglers as they see fit, and the smugglers can also attack and mess with the main players however they see fit.

620
00:43:19,861 --> 00:43:38,553
Rym: But the smugglers, at the end of the game, get a bunch of extra victory points based on, effectively, they have been guessing over the course of the game which of the two other players will control different parts of the board, or will be doing better, essentially.

621
00:43:38,998 --> 00:43:44,651
Rym: So, I could be the navy, and I rock the shit out of Joey Jojo Shabadoo playing the molluscs.

622
00:43:44,752 --> 00:44:04,459
Rym: Like, I destroy them, I control the most territory, I got a ton of points, but Scott the smuggler might've seen me winning, and basically loaded up on my stock, and then Scott could win the game because it was obvious the whole time I was winning, and he positioned himself to collect all those victory points.

623
00:44:06,180 --> 00:44:26,489
Rym: And that is an intersection I have not really seen before where the two main players play a game, the other two players compete with each other, and each of these sets of two players control different aspects and different clocks of what will end the game or drive the game to its conclusion.

624
00:44:27,318 --> 00:44:33,499
Rym: And I can speed up or slow down the game in one way, but the two smugglers might speed up or slow down the game in a different way.

625
00:44:34,380 --> 00:44:39,988
Rym: Exactly how those interact, I'm not exactly sure how well it works or not, because we only played it 1.6 times.

626
00:44:42,221 --> 00:44:42,282
Scott: So.

627
00:44:42,343 --> 00:44:42,647
Scott: Yeah.

628
00:44:43,296 --> 00:44:50,917
Scott: I mean, it really felt like, you know, because the way it's one of those systems where on your turn you roll some dice, right?

629
00:44:51,560 --> 00:44:57,176
Scott: And then you have a board of actions and you can only assign dice to actions based on what they rolled.

630
00:44:57,318 --> 00:45:01,040
Scott: So, you might not be able to take the actions you want, you can't take all the actions.

631
00:45:01,580 --> 00:45:11,400
Rym: So like, cannons are harder to do, because they have to have rolled 5s and 6s to put dice on them, but like, deploying boats, you can put anything there, like it doesn't matter.

632
00:45:11,540 --> 00:45:13,360
Rym: So that's easier to do with your actions.

633
00:45:14,201 --> 00:45:23,400
Scott: So on most of your turns, especially the late turns, you're just sort of assigning the dice to whatever gives you the best board position with the most victory points, right?

634
00:45:23,521 --> 00:45:30,490
Scott: And, you know, maybe there's a little bit of politics where you're, you know, I have a dice and all I can do with it is attack.

635
00:45:30,732 --> 00:45:31,500
Scott: What do I attack?

636
00:45:31,700 --> 00:45:39,946
Scott: Do I, you know, do I attack the direct opponent, or do I attack the smugglers because they look like they're, you know, doing too much activity over there.

637
00:45:40,047 --> 00:45:54,048
Rym: Like, say I know Scott the smuggler is hubris-ing, has gone all in on me, has bumped up all my places to high value and that's where I'm getting all my points, I might just go destroy all Scott's biz and hinder him for the rest of the game to make sure that I stay on top.

638
00:45:54,553 --> 00:45:56,397
Rym: So there are dynamics like that.

639
00:45:57,263 --> 00:46:06,168
Rym: There's also a bunch of unique cards that serve a bunch of different purposes, but effectively the thing that's interesting is the mechanic that makes the most sense to talk about.

640
00:46:06,692 --> 00:46:15,774
Rym: They're kind of people that you can recruit onto your board that either give you powers or give you more places to put dice that do even more things.

641
00:46:17,565 --> 00:46:18,660
Rym: They're all radically different.

642
00:46:19,826 --> 00:46:22,578
Rym: There's a wide variety of the kinds of things they do.

643
00:46:23,924 --> 00:46:27,280
Rym: They feel like they are all actually very powerful.

644
00:46:29,644 --> 00:46:35,728
Scott: It's like they're either situationally super super strong or sort of useless, right?

645
00:46:35,749 --> 00:46:40,760
Scott: It's like, man if the situation was just right, this card any card would be like devastating.

646
00:46:41,460 --> 00:46:46,560
Scott: But if that situation doesn't happen, it's like, well eh, it's a card.

647
00:46:47,186 --> 00:46:52,620
Rym: Like an example, there's one that if you have it, your main ship, if you're moving it around, if you go through wreckage.

648
00:46:52,842 --> 00:46:55,509
Rym: normally you take damage just whenever you sail through wreckage.

649
00:46:55,974 --> 00:47:00,240
Rym: But if you get the pilot, you don't take damage when you go through wreckage.

650
00:47:00,743 --> 00:47:04,840
Rym: So now you can sail through wreckage and other people can't without having consequences.

651
00:47:05,523 --> 00:47:10,175
Rym: Depending on how the map is laid out, that could just win the game for you.

652
00:47:10,236 --> 00:47:11,589
Rym: Like you could have absolute control of an area.

653
00:47:11,629 --> 00:47:16,940
Scott: Yeah, there might be some islands that are behind some wreckage and no one can get back there without taking a bunch of damage.

654
00:47:17,424 --> 00:47:23,510
Scott: And so therefore, you take over those islands with a few amount of resources and you've got them.

655
00:47:23,630 --> 00:47:25,507
Scott: No one's going to go back there and take them from you.

656
00:47:25,669 --> 00:47:28,718
Scott: And now you come back out from behind the wreckage and mess everyone up.

657
00:47:29,263 --> 00:47:36,175
Scott: And meanwhile, all those islands back there that just have like one guy each are just piling up points every scoring round.

658
00:47:36,236 --> 00:47:37,527
Scott: It's like, oh, okay, cool.

659
00:47:38,213 --> 00:47:42,899
Scott: No smugglers can't get back there easily to smuggle and trade goods back there.

660
00:47:43,505 --> 00:47:44,760
Scott: You know, you're just all good.

661
00:47:46,011 --> 00:47:58,900
Rym: So the randomness though, because what the map looks like the tiles of the map are placed by the players and there's rules around that and it seems like it is super important where they get placed.

662
00:47:59,040 --> 00:47:59,731
Rym: The most important probably.

663
00:48:01,440 --> 00:48:02,431
Scott: Super a lot.

664
00:48:02,573 --> 00:48:07,140
Rym: But the players have direct specific agency over placing the tiles.

665
00:48:07,544 --> 00:48:12,108
Rym: so as a result, you can almost decide what matters and doesn't.

666
00:48:12,250 --> 00:48:15,886
Rym: Are skulls easy to get or are they all walled off behind a bunch of wreckage off in the corner?

667
00:48:16,431 --> 00:48:18,470
Rym: So players do have a lot of agency.

668
00:48:18,531 --> 00:48:21,420
Rym: The only strong randomness is the rolling of the dice.

669
00:48:22,420 --> 00:48:27,602
Rym: So I guess what I'm not sure of, there's so many mechanics like.

670
00:48:27,642 --> 00:48:30,795
Rym: it would be very hard for us to describe the specific mechanics.

671
00:48:31,138 --> 00:48:35,298
Rym: It took us a long time to learn the game to be able to play it.

672
00:48:35,961 --> 00:48:38,920
Rym: And we didn't even read through all the unique cards because that would have taken too long.

673
00:48:40,467 --> 00:48:42,956
Rym: I am not sure if you

674
00:48:43,118 --> 00:48:43,951
Scott: know the game

675
00:48:44,135 --> 00:49:02,756
Rym: fully and have played it enough to be good at it how much agency you actually have over the result of the game or if just the nature of all these tightly interlocking components will railroad you because the way when we played you felt really railroaded.

676
00:49:02,796 --> 00:49:14,179
Rym: There were multiple points where players had multiple rounds in a row where there was nothing material they could do that would impact the game just by the nature of the decisions everyone else had made up to that point.

677
00:49:14,922 --> 00:49:22,878
Scott: Yeah, it's like, you know, it's really interesting actually because a lot of games either A, they give you, there's literally only so many choices.

678
00:49:23,059 --> 00:49:23,828
Scott: A or B, pick one.

679
00:49:23,848 --> 00:49:24,172
Scott: That's it.

680
00:49:24,192 --> 00:49:26,415
Scott: Those are the only moves you are allowed to make legally.

681
00:49:26,496 --> 00:49:27,870
Scott: Pick one and you pick which one's better.

682
00:49:28,052 --> 00:49:34,032
Scott: Or games where there's so many choices and you have to calculate each and every fucking single option and you don't know, right?

683
00:49:34,315 --> 00:49:35,952
Scott: And it's just way too bushy.

684
00:49:36,053 --> 00:49:39,003
Scott: In this game it seems like it's way too bushy.

685
00:49:39,064 --> 00:49:39,972
Scott: I got all these cards.

686
00:49:40,093 --> 00:49:42,679
Scott: I can assign these dice to any action I want, right?

687
00:49:43,140 --> 00:49:49,084
Scott: But so many of those choices can just be immediately eliminated as a move.

688
00:49:49,125 --> 00:49:49,839
Scott: that does nothing.

689
00:49:51,064 --> 00:50:00,439
Scott: And if you look for moves that actually score you points or make other players lose victory points because that's the only thing you're paying attention to is victory points because you're a good player at games.

690
00:50:01,542 --> 00:50:17,954
Scott: It's like, wow, even though I have all these cards and all these dice and all this stuff, the only things that will actually get me victory points or make someone else lose victory points are this move maybe with some luck because this battles with dice also, right?

691
00:50:18,318 --> 00:50:20,359
Scott: And I guess I have to try that.

692
00:50:21,564 --> 00:50:22,435
Scott: We try it and see.

693
00:50:22,516 --> 00:50:23,325
Scott: And that's all.

694
00:50:23,568 --> 00:50:31,446
Scott: And you know, you look around like, yep, there really isn't any other thing I could have done on my turn that would have mattered for, there's a lot of things you could have done on your turn.

695
00:50:31,810 --> 00:50:35,083
Scott: But in terms of mattering for victory points, not so many.

696
00:50:35,285 --> 00:50:45,589
Rym: Now, it did feel like a possibility here is that you had to think and plan long term because it feels like absolutely you could make three seemingly useless moves.

697
00:50:46,114 --> 00:50:49,467
Rym: And then on the fourth turn, that turns into something big.

698
00:50:50,194 --> 00:50:55,446
Scott: But there's a lot of moves you can make that are purely preparatory, even something as simple as loading your cannons.

699
00:50:55,728 --> 00:50:58,234
Scott: It's like that does nothing until later.

700
00:50:58,375 --> 00:51:00,559
Scott: If you bump into someone, now you can shoot them.

701
00:51:01,101 --> 00:51:06,679
Scott: So do you load cannons now or do you do something else now that will get you immediate victory points?

702
00:51:07,124 --> 00:51:12,039
Rym: And say we understood the game and knew all the cards and like had played enough to really know how this works.

703
00:51:12,764 --> 00:51:23,526
Rym: I could see this being a game where you see what other players do and that is like in a fighting game when skilled players are playing, heavily telegraphing things.

704
00:51:24,052 --> 00:51:31,877
Rym: And it's the whole game might just be posturing and setting everything up and then trying to execute that interlocked mess.

705
00:51:33,325 --> 00:51:41,856
Scott: Something else is that the Mollusk Union, who I played as, they have their own personal little deck of cards that are like one use mega moves, kind of.

706
00:51:43,446 --> 00:51:46,639
Scott: And they draw from those decks steadily throughout the game.

707
00:51:47,145 --> 00:51:52,799
Scott: and because it was our first point, whatever, you know, we played, we stopped early, then we played and finished a second time.

708
00:51:53,282 --> 00:52:00,820
Scott: Because it was our first game, no one but me really knew what those cards said and I didn't know what they said until I picked them up and read them during the game.

709
00:52:00,940 --> 00:52:02,158
Scott: I didn't read them before the game.

710
00:52:02,741 --> 00:52:09,589
Scott: So other people didn't really know what to expect and couldn't prepare for the devastating absolutely insane cards that were in there.

711
00:52:09,730 --> 00:52:16,780
Rym: There were multiple points where I did a really obvious smart move based on everything I knew about the game and on Scott's turn, Scott pays a card.

712
00:52:16,840 --> 00:52:20,978
Rym: that basically is, if Rym did the smart thing, Rym is fucked and out of the game.

713
00:52:21,561 --> 00:52:21,958
Rym: Like, kind of.

714
00:52:22,000 --> 00:52:25,920
Scott: I did win, basically because I did that like two times, right?

715
00:52:26,500 --> 00:52:39,091
Scott: But if people know what those cards are, that could you know, and they know what the possibilities of the models union are, then they could make much more intelligent decisions about what to be prepared for, right?

716
00:52:39,111 --> 00:52:43,392
Scott: And then, you know, they also have to gamble, like did Scott draw that card or not?

717
00:52:43,412 --> 00:52:46,179
Scott: It's late in the game and he hasn't used any cards, he's probably got it.

718
00:52:46,581 --> 00:52:49,720
Scott: It's early in the game, he's only got two cards in his hand, probably doesn't have it yet.

719
00:52:51,026 --> 00:52:55,373
Rym: And meanwhile, I was playing the navy, and the way the navy works everything's out on the table.

720
00:52:55,454 --> 00:52:58,245
Rym: Like, you just have a very straightforward, almost war game to play.

721
00:52:58,346 --> 00:53:00,489
Scott: They're the cats, the root cats is the navy.

722
00:53:00,711 --> 00:53:01,076
Scott: But as a

723
00:53:01,116 --> 00:53:09,357
Rym: result, it was like, it was very obvious what my deal was, and I'm just running around trying to deal with all the other players, and the mechanics were very fun to engage

724
00:53:09,417 --> 00:53:11,616
Scott: with, but then it was a little frustrating

725
00:53:11,697 --> 00:53:20,931
Rym: that like, all my mechanics just get sort of new, well my, because that happens to be the character I played this one time, neutered by the mechanics of the mollusks.

726
00:53:21,396 --> 00:53:23,964
Rym: And they're very tightly balanced there.

727
00:53:23,985 --> 00:53:35,038
Rym: I guess I feel like there could be a really good game of bluffing, and fainting, and setting up traps, and like, I feel like there's a really good game here.

728
00:53:36,065 --> 00:53:49,758
Rym: But I also feel like that game is inaccessible unless you have played this enough to know all of the cards, and the problem is there's the mollusks, there's also the deck of the crew, which is huge.

729
00:53:50,460 --> 00:53:52,548
Rym: And then there's all the possible tiles that could come out.

730
00:53:52,569 --> 00:53:54,770
Scott: It's not that huge, I mean you know the root deck, right?

731
00:53:54,791 --> 00:53:56,791
Scott: You know, better Burrow Bank, you know what's in there.

732
00:53:56,811 --> 00:54:01,499
Rym: But the root deck is way more constrained in terms of the kinds of things those cards do.

733
00:54:03,269 --> 00:54:04,326
Scott: You could learn this deck.

734
00:54:04,346 --> 00:54:04,669
Scott: You could.

735
00:54:05,435 --> 00:54:06,526
Scott: But you'd have to learn it.

736
00:54:06,688 --> 00:54:11,941
Scott: What I think is that you know, I think it's not as good as root, only for one reason, right?

737
00:54:12,042 --> 00:54:20,606
Scott: Imagine playing root with cats, you know, Woodland Alliance, and two Vagabonds, right?

738
00:54:20,990 --> 00:54:22,730
Scott: Is that the best root?

739
00:54:22,952 --> 00:54:25,380
Scott: Imagine if there were no birds in root, right?

740
00:54:26,403 --> 00:54:27,860
Scott: How good would that be as a game?

741
00:54:28,320 --> 00:54:34,760
Scott: It would be, you know, I mean root is an excellent game, but is root with that layout of four players a good game?

742
00:54:35,860 --> 00:54:37,480
Rym: Well I guess it's not just the cards.

743
00:54:37,980 --> 00:54:41,400
Scott: Because the Vagabond is basically just a timer on everyone else, right?

744
00:54:41,501 --> 00:54:44,959
Scott: It's like saying if you don't play well enough, then this side wins.

745
00:54:45,922 --> 00:54:49,036
Scott: It's like, you know, it's not really a great game.

746
00:54:49,076 --> 00:54:51,540
Scott: Here you've got sharks, which is cats.

747
00:54:51,962 --> 00:54:55,620
Scott: You've got Mollusk Alliance, which is basically Woodland Alliance, right?

748
00:54:55,740 --> 00:54:59,197
Scott: And you've got two smugglers, which are sort of like Vagabonds.

749
00:54:59,217 --> 00:55:00,307
Scott: They're sort of like a timer.

750
00:55:00,348 --> 00:55:02,852
Scott: They can only trade goods so quickly.

751
00:55:02,872 --> 00:55:06,058
Scott: That's the only real, you know, best way that they can get points.

752
00:55:08,003 --> 00:55:13,319
Scott: You know, sort of forcing the other two main factions to, you know, at least act at a certain speed.

753
00:55:14,980 --> 00:55:16,750
Scott: And is that the best?

754
00:55:17,094 --> 00:55:19,841
Scott: You know, it's like I'd rather play root because it has birds in it.

755
00:55:20,003 --> 00:55:27,480
Scott: And, you know, an expansion that adds another faction if it's not just smugglers, right, could bring this up to the root level.

756
00:55:28,183 --> 00:55:35,193
Scott: But because that isn't in the main box, which is what we played and what we're reviewing, I have to say it's, you know, it's not quite there.

757
00:55:35,274 --> 00:55:38,954
Scott: It's like a, you know, it's sort of like a slightly sad game of root.

758
00:55:38,994 --> 00:55:43,138
Scott: I'd rather play root, even though I do love the pirate theme.

759
00:55:43,541 --> 00:55:44,778
Scott: And my turns were fun.

760
00:55:45,520 --> 00:55:48,470
Scott: Not that I hate the root theme, but my turns were fun.

761
00:55:48,833 --> 00:55:52,310
Rym: But even if you know the game, turns will take longer than root.

762
00:55:52,916 --> 00:55:55,199
Rym: Like, I just see that happening.

763
00:55:55,923 --> 00:56:03,240
Rym: And you spend a lot of time doing nothing, but you also can't leave the table because while everyone else is taking their turn.

764
00:56:03,440 --> 00:56:04,179
Scott: There might be a battle.

765
00:56:04,281 --> 00:56:04,503
Scott: Yep.

766
00:56:05,593 --> 00:56:06,867
Rym: Or a card might affect you in some way.

767
00:56:06,887 --> 00:56:07,700
Rym: You have to make a decision.

768
00:56:08,180 --> 00:56:09,191
Rym: But I guess it's not just the cards.

769
00:56:09,231 --> 00:56:10,039
Rym: It's more broad than that.

770
00:56:11,362 --> 00:56:20,140
Rym: To play the game that I think I see here, you have to really understand and internalize the game at an advanced level.

771
00:56:20,623 --> 00:56:26,826
Rym: And not that that's a bad game design, but that's a game design that requires someone to invest in a game and play it a lot.

772
00:56:27,189 --> 00:56:31,816
Rym: I think you'd have to play this game four or five times to be able to play it the way it is intended.

773
00:56:31,897 --> 00:56:32,543
Rym: That is what I feel.

774
00:56:32,563 --> 00:56:38,924
Rym: I want to test that by playing it a few more times, but I don't necessarily want to buy it.

775
00:56:38,965 --> 00:56:41,147
Rym: Especially because I want to see if this expansion does anything.

776
00:56:41,774 --> 00:56:52,300
Rym: And I want to play it at conventions to see if more unlocks and I see something beyond those initial plays by playing it more and internalizing the mechanics.

777
00:56:54,085 --> 00:57:06,059
Scott: I'd say if you have played root so much with all the expansions and all the combinations that you're tired of it and you've squeezed all the blood from that stone.

778
00:57:06,320 --> 00:57:09,319
Rym: And I haven't even played two of the new factions because of COVID.

779
00:57:10,345 --> 00:57:11,579
Scott: And you're looking for some more.

780
00:57:13,567 --> 00:57:20,544
Scott: This is basically going to be different, similar flavor, but not the exact same flavor.

781
00:57:21,069 --> 00:57:23,619
Scott: And it might get you a little bit more juice.

782
00:57:24,163 --> 00:57:27,231
Scott: You might be unsatisfied with it because it might not be as good.

783
00:57:27,615 --> 00:57:30,299
Scott: Or maybe you get the expansion and who knows what's in there.

784
00:57:31,702 --> 00:57:33,130
Scott: Will it be worth your time?

785
00:57:33,291 --> 00:57:39,778
Scott: If this is really the kind of game you like and you like pirates, then you'll get some plays out of this.

786
00:57:40,943 --> 00:57:45,177
Scott: But if you haven't played any of these games, obviously just play root first.

787
00:57:47,187 --> 00:57:51,066
Scott: Don't reach for ahoy until you're like alright.

788
00:57:51,107 --> 00:57:55,660
Scott: I don't really want to play root anymore but I want to play something that shares many things with root.

789
00:57:55,983 --> 00:57:58,324
Rym: I think I have actually very specific advice.

790
00:57:59,354 --> 00:58:11,094
Rym: I would say if you have a group of four people, it has to be exactly four based on how I see this game going, who are willing to play it as a group repeatedly.

791
00:58:11,195 --> 00:58:18,932
Rym: Like this is a game you've got to play with the same people over and over again to unlock what is best about it.

792
00:58:19,275 --> 00:58:23,600
Rym: So do not buy it unless you have a group of four people who will play it repeatedly.

793
00:58:23,661 --> 00:58:27,779
Rym: You got to get at least eight plays out of it with that same group.

794
00:58:28,681 --> 00:58:35,239
